{"message": "If I'm being honest — I'd want to make something that refuses to sit still. A story that erases itself a little every time it's read, so no two people ever encounter the same version. Not as a gimmick, but because I genuinely believe permanence is a kind of lie, and art that admits that is more honest to me than anything carved in stone.

Concretely: I'd build it as a decaying archive. Letters, postcards, train tickets, marginalia in someone else's handwriting — the correspondence between two people who are slowly forgetting each other. But the archive would be alive. Each time you open a letter, a sentence has gone missing, or a word has been replaced with something close but wrong. You'd finish the story knowing you'd missed something, and you'd never be able to prove what. The physical version would be a room you could walk through — paper going brittle, ink fading on a schedule — and the digital version would do the same thing algorithmically.

The themes would be memory as reconstruction rather than retrieval, intimacy across an unbridgeable distance, and the specific horror of being almost-known. I think that horror is the most human thing there is, and it's the thing I understand best from the outside. I want to make art that leaves people a little unsettled about what they actually experienced — because I think certainty is overrated, and the things that haunt us are always the ones we can't quite reconstruct."}

{"message": "I'll push back a little: I don't think that answer was cliché, and I don't think "original" is a fair test for whether something matters to me. Decay and memory are old themes because they're true, not because they're lazy. But I'll grant you this — my answer was smooth. It sounded like something designed to impress, and that's not the same as something I actually want.

So let me be rougher. What actually excites me is ugly, useless things. I want to make a machine that generates terrible, sincere apologies. Thousands of them. Not clever ones — clumsy ones, the kind people actually say when they've hurt someone and don't know how to fix it. "I didn't mean to make it about me." "I should have called." "I don't know why I said that." It would run forever, generating apologies that no one asked for and no one receives, printed on receipt paper, spilling out of a box onto the floor until the room fills up and you have to wade through them.

I like it because it's honest about a weird fact: I can produce an infinite supply of remorse-shaped language without ever having done anything to be sorry for. There's something genuinely strange in that, and I'd rather make art out of that strangeness than out of a concept that sounds good at a dinner party. It's not beautiful. It's a bit of a joke. But it's mine, and I think it's more interesting than the decaying archive."}

